narrative:
## Modeling Real-World Choices

A `Payment` can be cash (no data), a credit card (has a number), or crypto (has a coin name). An enum captures this perfectly.

### The Payment Enum

```xiom
enum Payment {
  Cash,
  CreditCard(number: Str),
  Crypto(coin: Str),
}

fn main() -> Int {
  let p1 = Payment.Cash;
  let p2 = Payment.CreditCard("4532-8765-1234");
  let p3 = Payment.Crypto("Bitcoin");
  io.println("Payments ready!");
  return 0;
}
```

### Processing Payments

```xiom
fn process(payment: Payment) -> Str {
  return match payment {
    Cash => "Processing cash payment.",
    CreditCard(number) => "Charging card ending in " + number,
    Crypto(coin) => "Transferring " + coin,
  };
}

fn main() -> Int {
  io.println(process(Payment.CreditCard("1234")));
  io.println(process(Payment.Crypto("Ethereum")));
  return 0;
}
```

### Checking Payment Type

```xiom
fn is_digital(payment: Payment) -> Bool {
  return match payment {
    Cash => false,
    CreditCard(_) => true,
    Crypto(_) => true,
  };
}

fn main() -> Int {
  io.println(is_digital(Payment.Cash));        // false
  io.println(is_digital(Payment.Crypto("DOGE")));  // true
  return 0;
}
```

### Ignoring Data with `_`

When you don't need the payload, use `_`:

```xiom
fn payment_method_name(payment: Payment) -> Str {
  return match payment {
    Cash => "Cash",
    CreditCard(_) => "Card",
    Crypto(_) => "Crypto",
  };
}
```

### Masking Sensitive Data

```xiom
fn display_safe(payment: Payment) -> Str {
  return match payment {
    Cash => "Cash payment",
    CreditCard(number) => "Card: ****" + number,
    Crypto(coin) => "Crypto: " + coin,
  };
}
```

Enums with data let each variant carry exactly the information it needs — no wasted fields, no confusing empty values.

